[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Introduction to SOLID and Coupling vs Cohesion"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Welcome to this technical tutorial on foundational software architecture concepts. Writing code that works is only the first step in a developer\'s journey. Writing code that is maintainable, extensible, and robust requires a deep understanding of core architectural principles, specifically Coupling, Cohesion, and the SOLID design principles."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding Cohesion"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Cohesion refers to the degree to which the elements inside a module belong together. In a highly cohesive system, responsibilities are closely related, and the module performs a single, well-defined task."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"High Cohesion: A class named \'InvoiceCalculator\' that only calculates taxes, discounts, and final totals for an invoice."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Low Cohesion: A class named \'InvoiceManager\' that calculates totals, saves to the database, prints a PDF, and sends an email notification."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding Coupling"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"Coupling measures the degree of interdependence between software modules. Low coupling means that changing one module requires little to no change in other modules."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Tight (High) Coupling: Classes directly instantiate their dependencies using the \'new\' keyword, making unit testing and substitution difficult."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Loose (Low) Coupling: Classes interact through abstractions (interfaces), allowing implementations to be swapped seamlessly."},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"The Golden Rule of Software Design"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"As a rule of thumb, strive for high cohesion and low coupling. This architectural balance ensures that your codebase remains flexible and resilient to changing business requirements."},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Introduction to SOLID Principles"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"Coined by Robert C. Martin (Uncle Bob), the SOLID acronym represents five design principles intended to make software designs more understandable, flexible, and maintainable:"},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"S - Single Responsibility Principle (SRP)"},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"O - Open/Closed Principle (OCP)"},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"L - Liskov Substitution Principle (LSP)"},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"I - Interface Segregation Principle (ISP)"},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"D - Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP)"},{"order":20,"type":"subheading","text":"Code Example: Tight Coupling vs Loose Coupling"},{"order":21,"type":"paragraph","text":"Consider the following tightly coupled implementation where the Order class directly creates a MySQL database instance."},{"order":22,"type":"code","text":"class MySqlDatabase {
    public void save(String data) {
        // Saving to MySQL
    }
}

class OrderProcessor {
    private MySqlDatabase db = new MySqlDatabase();

    public void process(String orderData) {
        db.save(orderData);
    }
}"},{"order":23,"type":"paragraph","text":"To decouple this architecture and adhere to SOLID principles, we introduce an interface, allowing us to pass any database implementation."},{"order":24,"type":"code","text":"interface Database {
    void save(String data);
}

class MongoDatabase implements Database {
    public void save(String data) {
        // Saving to MongoDB
    }
}

class OrderProcessor {
    private Database db;

    public OrderProcessor(Database db) {
        this.db = db;
    }

    public void process(String orderData) {
        db.save(orderData);
    }
}"},{"order":25,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":26,"type":"paragraph","text":"Mastering coupling, cohesion, and the SOLID principles transforms you from a code writer into a software architect. In the upcoming modules, we will deep-dive into each individual SOLID principle with comprehensive examples."}]
